Done. `/app/solution.sparql` is written and verified.

## The query

```sparql
SELECT ?professorName (GROUP_CONCAT(DISTINCT ?country; separator=", ") AS ?countries)
WHERE {
  ?professor a uni:Person ; uni:hasName ?professorName ; uni:role ?role ; uni:worksIn ?dept .
  ?dept uni:belongsTo ?university . ?university uni:locatedInCountry ?country .
  FILTER ( STRSTARTS( ?role, "Professor" ) )                 # 1 full professor
  FILTER EXISTS { ... ?euCountry IN (27 EU codes) }          # 2 EU department
  { SELECT ?professor (COUNT(DISTINCT ?student) AS ?currentStudents)
    WHERE { ?professor uni:worksIn ?busyDept . ?course uni:isTaughtIn ?busyDept .
            ?student uni:isEnrolledIn ?course .
            OPTIONAL { ?student uni:hasGraduationDate ?graduationDate }
            FILTER ( !BOUND(?graduationDate) || ?graduationDate > "2025-08-16"^^xsd:date ) }
    GROUP BY ?professor ?busyDept HAVING ( COUNT(DISTINCT ?student) > 10 ) }   # 3
}
GROUP BY ?professor ?professorName ORDER BY ?professorName
```

Design points: `STRSTARTS(?role,"Professor")` admits full professors while excluding `"Assistant Professor …"`; the country for `?countries` is collected in the main pattern over *all* `worksIn` departments, so non-EU posts still appear; criterion 3 is a group subselect counting distinct students per department (a student enrolled in two courses in the same department counts once).

## Result on the given graph

| ?professorName | ?countries |
|---|---|
| Aristotle Tympas | GR |
| Orfeas Menis | GR, US |
| Pepe Attanasio | GR, PT |

Excluded, with reasons: Marie Dubois (Sorbonne has only 6 current students — 8 of the 14 enrolled in Math201 graduated before 2025-08-16), John Smith (MIT, no EU department), Athanasios Voulodimos and Stefan Mueller (assistant professors). Current-enrollment counts: ComputerScience_NTUA 12, HistoryAndPhilosophy_NKUA 12, Engineering_Berkeley 11, Biology_MIT 7, Mathematics_Sorbonne 6.

## Verification

No SPARQL engine was available and the sandbox has no network (`rdflib`/`pip` absent, `curl` blocked by policy), so I parsed the Turtle and evaluated the query clause-by-clause in Python, twice with independently written checkers. Both reproduced the 3 rows above. The checkers also assert against the file on disk: all 21 expected graph patterns present, the `IN` list is exactly the EU-27 (no `CH`, no `US`, no omissions), and the reference date `2025-08-16` is used. Throwaway scripts were deleted, leaving only `solution.sparql`.

Two data quirks worth knowing: `Student5` references an undefined `uni:Bio10` (it joins nothing, so it silently contributes no enrollment — it does not affect any count here), and the file uses CRLF line endings.

